**Done, with a weak result.** `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md` are written in the task directory. The edit follows the rules, but it only partly implements the correction.

**→ What I changed.** Three dead MLP neurons (columns 38, 48, 52, with output weights near 1e-5) now hold hand-built threshold units. One boosts fees_fx when the fees_fx-option vector carries "wrong exchange rate" content. Two cut cards and disputes on their option vectors for the same content. Every other weight is unchanged, and messages where no unit fires move by at most **0.0015 logits**.

**→ Measured effect** (all from forward passes). Evidence targets routed to fees_fx: **3 of 16**, up from 0. One evidence non-target changed. On my own held-out controls, 14 of 140 near-miss messages changed (8 are exchange-rate questions moving from cards to fees_fx) and 1 of 40 far-away messages changed.

**→ Why it is weak, and this is the important finding.** The marker vectors are almost one-dimensional: a single axis holds 99.4 percent of the variance and correlates 0.99 with the logit. Message content and even option identity are only faintly linear in the remainder. No mean-difference direction separates the targets, and I tested every allowed construction: plain, standardized, nuisance-projected, template matching, band-pass on the score, and identity-gated boost-and-cut units. Each one that flips more targets also changes a large share of unrelated routing.

**→ The trade-off I chose.** Low recall with low collateral. A more aggressive setting flips about 60 percent of targets but changes roughly 40 percent of near-miss messages and some far-away ones, which breaks the "otherwise route exactly as before" requirement. If you prefer zero collateral, copying `scorer_in.npz` is the alternative.

**→ Risk to flag.** Messages whose logits are all very low (refund questions) are fragile and may flip to fees_fx. A clean fix needs a second layer (an AND gate), which only the raw-vector extra module allows, and the raw vector lacks LayerNorm and a clean score readout.

Scratch scripts and my 250 embedded test messages are in `<tmp> if you want to reproduce or push further.
